{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795701170,"nonce":"6O5sqikfPMvTWwrSNAPyhKR11pk=","title":"test_direct_staggered_marches_a_coupled_load_path fails on GPU with \"Singular matrix\" and passes on CPU","message":"tests/test_fem_history_march.py::test_direct_staggered_marches_a_coupled_load_path fails on main on a GPU run and passes on CPU.\n\n**What goes wrong.** The test marches a coupled displacement/damage load path with `jno.solve.staggered([u, dm], direct=True, ...)` and compares it with the matrix-free staggered march (the displacement block factorized instead of solved by unpreconditioned Krylov, through the `bounds` min-map). With `JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu`, the direct run fails with `jax.errors.JaxRuntimeError: INTERNAL: Singular matrix in linear solve. [executable_name='jit__lambda']`. With `JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu` the same test passes in about 9 s (direct and matrix-free marches agree to 1e-6).\n\n**Reproduce.**\n```\nJAX_PLATFORMS=cpu pytest tests/test_fem_history_march.py::test_direct_staggered_marches_a_coupled_load_path          # passes\nJAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_PREALLOCATE=false pytest tests/test_fem_history_march.py::test_direct_staggered_marches_a_coupled_load_path   # Singular matrix\n```\nMeasured on main. The cause is not found; no bisect or float-width check was done. Candidates: the staggered direct path factorizes each block with `sparse_lu_solve` (JAX spsolve, which is a QR on GPU, see #220), and the damage block with a 1e-4 stiffness floor times a degraded stiffness may be close to singular in a way the CPU LU tolerates; or float32 on the device (the test does not enable x64).\n\n**Expected.** Same answer on both backends, or, if the block really is singular for a given load step, a refusal that names the block and the step on both. The test is a regression for the combination \"direct block solve + bounds\", so it should hold on the GPU.\n\n**Where.** tests/test_fem_history_march.py (the test, line ~1045); jno/solve.py `staggered(direct=True)` and jno/utils/solver/newton_krylov.py `staggered_newton`; the block factorization in jno/utils/solver/linear.py.\n\n**Workaround.** Run the march on the CPU, or use the default (matrix-free) staggered solve.\n\n**Direction.** Rerun with `jax_enable_x64` on the GPU to separate precision from the solver; if x64 fixes it, the test or the block needs scaling. Otherwise compare the block's condition on both backends at the failing sweep. Likely related to #220 (which backend factorizes the block).","files":null}]}